<section class="bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 py-16">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-6 text-center"><%= post.title %></h1>
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 text-center mb-10">Published on <time datetime="<%= post.date %>"><%= post.date %></time> · Updated on <time datetime="<%= post.dateModified %>"><%= post.dateModified %></time> · By <%= post.author %></p>

    <%- include('../partials/ad-unit', { adSlot: 'blog-540p-top' }) %>

    <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
      <p class="text-lg text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        You have probably seen 480p, 720p, and 1080p in video quality menus, but 540p is less common. It is a middle resolution that sits between standard definition and high definition. This article explains what 540p is, where it appears, and why platforms sometimes use it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">What Is 540p?</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">540p usually has a resolution of 960 × 540 pixels. It is exactly half the width and half the height of 1080p, which makes it easy to scale. Because it is one-quarter of the pixels of 1080p, file sizes are much smaller while still looking noticeably sharper than 480p.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">Why Do Some Platforms Use 540p?</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">540p is a compromise. It gives a clearer picture than 480p without the bandwidth cost of 720p. Some video players and older smartphones handle 540p better than higher resolutions. It is also useful when storage space is limited but the viewer still wants a sharper image than standard definition.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">540p vs 480p and 720p</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">540p offers a good middle ground:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
        <li><strong>480p (854 × 480):</strong> smaller file, softer image</li>
        <li><strong>540p (960 × 540):</strong> sharper than 480p, still small file</li>
        <li><strong>720p (1280 × 720):</strong> much clearer, but uses more data and processing</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mt-4">For phone screens, 540p can look close to 720p, while saving data. On larger screens, 720p or 1080p is usually better.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">Common Uses for 540p</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">You might see 540p in these situations:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
        <li>Mobile streaming on slow or limited data connections</li>
        <li>Previews or trailers where file size must be small</li>
        <li>Older devices that struggle with higher resolutions</li>
        <li>Archiving content when storage is limited</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">File Size and Bandwidth</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">A one-minute H.264 MP4 video at 540p is roughly 10–25 MB, depending on bitrate. That is larger than 480p but smaller than 720p. Streaming 540p typically needs 1.5–3 Mbps for smooth playback.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">Should You Use 540p?</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">If your connection is slow, your screen is small, or you need to save storage, 540p is a smart choice. It gives a cleaner image than 480p without the heavy demands of 720p. For larger screens or editing, stick with 1080p or higher.</p>
    </div>

    <%- include('../partials/ad-unit', { adSlot: 'blog-540p-bottom' }) %>

    <div class="mt-10 text-center">
      <a href="/blog" class="btn-primary">Back to all guides</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
